# Token scope check

The nx-fhir `auth-scopes` feature makes this server check the scopes of the token of a FHIR request. A request gets status 403 when no scope of the token covers its resource type and its action. The check applies to each verified token. It does not need the `auth-smart` feature or the `auth-udap` feature.

The scope syntax follows SMART App Launch 2.2.0. The check uses the rule engine of the HAPI FHIR `AuthorizationInterceptor`.

The feature needs the `auth-resource` feature. It uses `app.auth.enabled` and the verified claims that `BearerTokenInterceptor` of that feature keeps on each request. When the server has no `auth-resource`, the install offers to install it first. In an interactive run, the install asks one question, and yes is the default. With yes, `auth-resource` installs first and asks its own questions. In a run that is not interactive, `auth-resource` installs with its defaults, and the install asks no question.

## What the feature adds

| File | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `src/main/java/<%= packagePath %>/scopes/ScopeConfig.java` | Registers the scope check on the FHIR server. |
| `src/main/java/<%= packagePath %>/scopes/ScopeProperties.java` | The configuration properties under `app.auth.scopes`. |
| `src/main/java/<%= packagePath %>/scopes/ScopeAuthorizationInterceptor.java` | Builds the rules of each request from the scopes of the token and answers a refused request. |
| `src/main/java/<%= packagePath %>/scopes/SmartScope.java` | Reads the resource scopes of the `scope` claim. |
| `src/test/java/<%= packagePath %>/scopes/SmartScopeTest.java` | Unit test of the scope forms. |
| `src/test/java/<%= packagePath %>/scopes/AuthScopesIT.java` | Integration test of the requests with the token check and the scope check on. It runs under `mvn verify`. |
| `src/main/resources/application.yaml` | The `app.auth.scopes` entries. |
| `src/test/resources/config/application.yaml` | `app.auth.scopes.enabled: false` for each Spring Boot test. |
| `src/test/resources/application-test.yaml` | `app.auth.scopes.enabled: false` for a test with the `test` profile. |

The two test configuration files turn the feature off. A test that sets `app.auth.enabled` and `app.auth.scopes.enabled` to `true` runs with the feature.

## Configuration

With the defaults of the two features, the install writes this configuration:

```yaml
app:
  auth:
    enabled: true
    issuer: https://udap-security.fast.hl7.org
    public-endpoints: []
    ssl-verify: true
    # bypass-header: X-Bypass-Auth
    scopes:
      enabled: true
```

The `auth-scopes` install writes the `scopes` map. The `auth-resource` install writes the other keys of `app.auth`.

### `app.auth.scopes.enabled`

- `app.auth.enabled` and `app.auth.scopes.enabled` are both `true`: the feature registers the interceptor, and the server checks the scopes.
- One of the two is `false` or absent: the feature registers no interceptor, and the server checks no scope.

## Which requests get the check

- A request with verified claims gets the check, other than a direct request for an operation (see [Operations](#operations)). The claims come from `BearerTokenInterceptor` of the `auth-resource` feature.
- A request with no verified claims passes with no check. `BearerTokenInterceptor` reads no token for these requests, so they have no verified claims also when they have a token: `metadata`, a request under `/fhir/.well-known/`, a public endpoint of `app.auth.public-endpoints`, a request with the bypass header, and a `_getpages` request.

## Scope forms

The server reads the claim `scope`. The claim is one string with spaces between the scopes, or a JSON list of such strings.

A resource scope has the form `(patient|user|system)/(Type|*).(permissions)`:

- `Type` is a resource type of the FHIR version of the server. `*` is each type.
- `permissions` has one or more of the letters `cruds` in that order, or `read` (the same as `rs`), `write` (the same as `cud`), or `*` (the same as `cruds`).

The server ignores each other scope, for example `openid`, `fhirUser`, `launch/patient`, `offline_access`, and `udap`. A resource scope in a different form grants nothing. Examples are a scope with a `?` suffix, letters in a different order, a different prefix, and a type that the server does not have. The scopes of a token add up.

For a `user/` or `system/` scope, the check reads resource types and does not read search parameters. A `patient/` scope also limits the request to one Patient compartment (see [Prefixes and the `limit` method](#prefixes-and-the-limit-method)).

## Letters

This section applies to `user/` and `system/` scopes. For a `patient/` scope, see [Prefixes and the `limit` method](#prefixes-and-the-limit-method).

| Letter | Direct requests |
| --- | --- |
| `c` | create, conditional create |
| `r` | read, vread, instance history |
| `u` | update, patch, conditional update. An update that makes a new resource passes with `u`. |
| `d` | delete, conditional delete |
| `s` | type search, type history, system history |

- A request for a type with no covering scope gets status 403 on the incoming request. This is also true when the search has no match or the id does not exist.
- A conditional delete for a type with no covering scope gets status 403 when it matches a resource, and the resource stays. When it matches no resource, it gets status 200 and deletes nothing.
- A conditional create that matches a stored resource gets status 200 and returns that resource. It needs `c` on the type and no `r` or `s`.
- A create or an update can hold a reference in the form of a search, for example `"reference": "Encounter?identifier=123"`. The server puts the id of the resource that matches in its place, and the token needs no scope on that type. A direct create or update returns the id in its response. With no match, the request gets status 404. With more than one match, it gets status 412.
- Each resource in a response needs the letter of its request on its own type. A resource that a search adds through `_include` or `_revinclude` needs `s` on its type. One resource with no covering scope makes the complete request fail with status 403.
- A system history needs `s` on each type that it returns.
- A write needs no `r` for the resource in its response.
- A page of a search (`_getpages`) gets no check. `BearerTokenInterceptor` lets a paging request through before it reads a token, so such a request never has claims. The include rule above applies to the first page only.

## Transactions and batches

- The server checks each entry with the same rules.
- One write entry with no covering scope refuses the complete Bundle with status 403. This is also true for a batch, with one exception: in a batch, an update that makes a new resource with `u` and no `c` gets a 403 entry in a 200 Bundle.
- A read entry with no covering scope gets a 403 entry in a 200 Bundle.
- A transaction or a batch with a create entry (`POST`) that has `ifNoneExist` gets status 403 for the complete Bundle when only a `patient/` create scope of the token covers the type of the entry. For a transaction, this is also true when the condition matches no resource. A create entry with `ifNoneExist` of a type that a `user/` create scope covers follows the `user/` rule.
- In a Bundle, an update that makes a new resource needs `u` and `c` on its type. The rule engine checks the entry as an update on the request and as a create before the write.
- An operation as an entry of a Bundle needs `r` or `s` on each type in its response, and one type with no covering scope makes the complete Bundle fail with status 403.
- In a Bundle with `Prefer: return=representation`, a created resource needs `r` or `s`.

## Operations

A direct request for an operation (`$name`) gets no scope check, also for the reads and writes that the operation makes. For example, a token with only `patient/Patient.r` can call `$everything`, `$expunge`, and `$reindex`.

`buildRuleList` of `ScopeAuthorizationInterceptor` returns a rule list that allows all for a direct request for an operation. The rule `allow().operation().withAnyName().atAnyLevel().andAllowAllResponses()` passes an operation as an entry of a Bundle. To check operations, change these two places.

## Prefixes and the `limit` method

A `user/` or `system/` scope covers each resource of its type. A `patient/` scope covers the resources of its type in the Patient compartment of the patient that the `patient` claim of the token names. The claim value is a resource id with no type, for example `123`.

- With no `patient` claim that is a string with text, a `patient/` scope grants nothing.
- A `patient/` scope on a type with no resource in the Patient compartment, for example Practitioner, allows no read, search, create, update, or delete. A conditional delete that matches no resource gets status 200 and deletes nothing.

With a `patient/` scope, the letters allow these direct requests:

| Letter | Direct requests |
| --- | --- |
| `c` | create of a resource in the compartment. A conditional create (`If-None-Exist`) gets status 403 when only a `patient/` create scope of the token covers the type, also when the condition matches a resource of the patient or no resource. |
| `r` | read and vread of a resource in the compartment, and instance history of the Patient of the claim |
| `u` | update of a resource in the compartment, also an update that makes a new resource, and patch of the Patient of the claim. A patch of a different resource gets status 403. |
| `d` | delete of a resource in the compartment |
| `s` | type search that names the patient in a parameter, for example `Observation?patient=123`. A system history gets status 403. |

With a `patient/` scope, these requests get an answer about a resource of a different patient:

- A search that names the patient of the claim and a different patient in one parameter, for example `Observation?patient=123,456`, gets status 403. With `_summary=count` or `_count=0`, it gets status 200 and a total for the two patients.
- A conditional delete that matches a resource of a different patient gets status 403, and the resource stays. With no match, it gets status 200.
- A create or an update with a reference in the form of a search, for example `Encounter?identifier=123`, gets the id of the resource that matches, also when it is a resource of a different patient.

The method `limit` of `ScopeAuthorizationInterceptor` returns the resources that one scope covers:

```java
protected IAuthRuleBuilderRuleOpClassifierFinished limit(
		IAuthRuleBuilderRuleOpClassifier rule, SmartScope scope, Map<String, Object> claims) {
	if (!"patient".equals(scope.prefix())) {
		return rule.withAnyId();
	}
	if (!(claims.get("patient") instanceof String patient) || patient.isBlank()) {
		return null;
	}
	IIdType owner = context.getVersion().newIdType().setParts(null, "Patient", patient, null);
	// The one-owner form adds to an existing rule of the same operation, type, and compartment, and both testers apply.
	return rule.inCompartment("Patient", List.of(owner));
}
```

- `context` is the `FhirContext` of the server. `ScopeConfig` gives it to the constructor.
- With the result `null`, the scope grants nothing.
- The method runs one time for each rule of a scope (read, write, create, and delete). It gets the scope, not the letters of the rule.
- `claims` holds the verified claims of the token.

To limit a scope in a different way, change this method. To make a different rule list, change `buildRuleList`.

The patient limit refuses a request, and it does not filter a response. A search that names the patient in a parameter passes, for example `Observation?patient=123` or `Patient?_id=123`. A search with no parameter that names the patient gets status 403 when no `user/` or `system/` scope of the token covers its type.

The patient limit does not apply to a direct request for an operation, because such a request gets no scope check. With a `patient/` scope, `$everything` for a different patient passes.

## The answer for a missing scope

A refused request gets:

- status 403
- the header `WWW-Authenticate: Bearer error="insufficient_scope"` (RFC 6750)
- an `OperationOutcome` with severity `error`, the code `forbidden`, and the fixed text `The token does not allow this request`

The text names no rule and no scope.
